"89_apptoolwin.mst — AppSpec A1: the lifecycle and the reconcile loop, PORTABLE.

 `docs/appspec.md` is the decision record; this is stage A1 of its ladder.

 WINARMVM keeps all of this in one class, `WinToolWindow`, mixed together with
 the Win32 that draws it. WE SPLIT IT, and the split is the reason a tool
 written on either platform runs on both: everything in this file is the
 contract — the registry, the dispatch discipline, the counters, the damage
 bookkeeping, and the republish -> validate -> diff -> ops pipeline — and
 everything platform-shaped is behind a REALIZER, one instance per window,
 which this file only ever talks to through nine messages.

 THE REALIZER PROTOCOL, in full, so a new platform knows exactly what it owes:

     newFor: aWindow          answer a realizer bound to that window
     open                     create-or-front the native window; answer Boolean
     hide                     make it invisible, keep the state (close = hide)
     teardown                 destroy natives, in the platform's own order
     isOpen                   Boolean
     metrics                  {charWidth. lineHeight} of the window's font
     clientWidth              pixels available to lay out in
     destroyControls          drop every realized control (a rebuild)
     createFor: aPlacement    {node. x. y. w. h. kind} -> one native control
     applyNodeProps: c to: id patch one node's changed props in place
     applyWindowProps: c      patch the window's own props (title, so far)
     paneHandleFor: id        the surface handle a pane node was realized into
     layoutFinished: places   every control is placed; fit to them if you can

 ONE OBLIGATION THAT IS EASY TO MISS, written here because it cost a bug in
 the first realizer and will cost one in the next: whatever a realizer holds
 to represent a node must be BROUGHT UP TO DATE as it applies a patch. Most
 props are set straight from the patch and it does not matter, but a choice
 control is refilled from its NODE (a selection is an index into that node's
 options), so a realizer that kept the node it was handed at creation time
 refills with yesterday's answer — the demo's colour popup still reading `Red`
 after the model had moved to Blue, with the patch correct and the screen
 wrong. It is the realizer's half of the host-echo rule: what it holds must
 match what is on screen.

 THE AUTHOR OF A TOOL SEES NONE OF THAT. A tool is a title, a block that
 answers a spec and a block that handles an event — no window code, no control
 code, no layout code, no teardown code. That is the whole point of the file,
 and it is what `docs/appspec.md` means by the realizer owning the memory
 management: you cannot leak a native object you never held.

 THE DISPATCH DISCIPLINE is winscheme's, and it is why a handler that changes
 five things costs one screen update: event depth is COUNTED; a `rerender`
 asked for inside a handler only raises a flag; the one coalesced reconcile
 runs when depth returns to zero. While ops are applied, events are
 SUPPRESSED — a programmatic update that comes back as a user event is a loop,
 and it is the trap every declarative UI over a native toolkit meets.

 THE COUNTERS ARE NOT DECORATION. `patchCount` and `rebuildCount` are how an
 id-strategy failure is SEEN rather than felt as flicker: a tool that rebuilds
 its window on every keystroke still looks correct in a screenshot. The demo
 tool puts them on its own face for exactly that reason."

"────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 The null realizer: the whole pipeline, no platform.

 It is not a stub for tests to tolerate — it is the correct behaviour for an
 image with no display, and it is what makes A1's claims checkable before A2
 exists. It RECORDS what a real realizer would have done (the placements it
 was handed, the patches it was asked to apply), which is exactly the evidence
 a test about a differ wants: `one click produced one setNodeProps naming
 clicks` is a statement about the ops, and needs no window to be true."
Object subclass: AppNullRealizer [
    | window open placements patches windowPatches panes |

    AppNullRealizer class >> newFor: aWindow <Object> ^ <AppNullRealizer> [
        ^self new setWindow: aWindow
    ]

    setWindow: aWindow <Object> ^ <AppNullRealizer> [
        window := aWindow.
        open := false.
        placements := OrderedCollection new.
        patches := OrderedCollection new.
        windowPatches := OrderedCollection new.
        panes := Dictionary new.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the realizer protocol ───────────────────────────────────────────"

    open ^ <Boolean> [ open := true. ^true ]
    hide [ open := false. ^self ]
    teardown [ open := false. self destroyControls. ^self ]
    isOpen ^ <Boolean> [ ^open ]

    "ROUND NUMBERS ON PURPOSE. A test that asserts exact pixels wants metrics
     it can do arithmetic with in its head; the Cocoa realizer measures its
     real font instead."
    metrics ^ <Array> [ ^AppSpecLayout metricsCharWidth: 8 lineHeight: 16 ]
    clientWidth ^ <Integer> [ ^window width ]

    destroyControls [
        panes do: [ :h | AppSurfaces remove: h ].
        panes := Dictionary new.
        placements := OrderedCollection new.
        ^self
    ]

    createFor: aPlacement <Array> [
        | node w h |
        placements add: aPlacement.
        "A PANE GETS A REAL SURFACE EVEN HEADLESS, with a present that does
         nothing. That is what lets a paint block — the part of a tool most
         likely to be wrong — be driven and its pixels READ BACK in a test,
         with no window anywhere."
        node := aPlacement at: 1.
        ((aPlacement at: 6) == #control
            and: [ AppSpec paneTypes includes: node type ]) ifTrue: [
                w := ((node at: #width) ifNil: [ 320 ]) rounded.
                h := ((node at: #height) ifNil: [ 180 ]) rounded.
                panes at: node idOrEmpty put:
                    (AppSurfaces register: w height: h present: [ :s | nil ]) ].
        ^self
    ]

    paneHandleFor: anId <Object> ^ <Integer> [
        ^panes at: anId asString ifAbsent: [ 0 ]
    ]

    applyNodeProps: changed <OrderedCollection> to: nodeId <String> [
        patches add: (Array with: nodeId with: changed).
        ^self
    ]

    applyWindowProps: changed <OrderedCollection> [
        windowPatches add: changed.
        ^self
    ]

    layoutFinished: places <Object> [ ^self ]

    tick [ ^self ]

    "── what a test reads ───────────────────────────────────────────────"

    placements ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^placements ]
    patches ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^patches ]
    windowPatches ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^windowPatches ]

    "The nodes actually realized, by id — `controls` in spirit, without a
     control in sight."
    placedIds ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | out |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        placements do: [ :p | ((p at: 6) == #control) ifTrue: [ out add: (p at: 1) idOrEmpty ] ].
        ^out
    ]

    placementFor: anId <Object> kind: aKind <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        placements do: [ :p |
            (((p at: 1) idOrEmpty = anId asString) and: [ (p at: 6) == aKind ])
                ifTrue: [ ^p ] ].
        ^nil
    ]
]

Object subclass: AppToolWindow [
    <classVars: Windows Order EventDepth RerenderPending Suppress
                PatchCount RebuildCount RealizerClass Reports RegisterHook>

    "── the registry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    AppToolWindow class >> windows ^ <Dictionary> [
        Windows isNil ifTrue: [ Windows := Dictionary new ].
        ^Windows
    ]

    "REGISTRATION ORDER, kept beside the dictionary and not derived from it.
     A Dictionary has no order, so a Tools menu built by iterating one would
     hand the same tool a different position from run to run — invisible until
     something drives the menu by index and opens the wrong window."
    AppToolWindow class >> order ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        Order isNil ifTrue: [ Order := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Order
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> register: anId <Symbol> title: aTitle <String>
                           width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer>
                           render: renderBlock <Object> handler: handlerBlock <Object> ^ <Object> [
        ^self register: anId title: aTitle width: w height: h
              render: renderBlock handler: handlerBlock paint: nil
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> register: anId <Symbol> title: aTitle <String>
                           width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer>
                           render: renderBlock <Object> handler: handlerBlock <Object>
                           paint: paintBlock <Object> ^ <Object> [
        "THE PAINT BLOCK IS FOR PANES AND ONLY FOR PANES. A control's contents
         are its props and the differ carries those; a pane's contents are
         PIXELS the tool computes, which no diff of a spec could express. So
         the spec PLACES a pane and this block FILLS it.

         Declared, not built: a tool that is never opened costs a dictionary
         entry and no window, which is what makes it reasonable to register
         every tool at load time."
        | tool |
        tool := AppToolWindow new
            setId: anId title: aTitle width: w height: h
            render: renderBlock handler: handlerBlock paint: paintBlock.
        (self windows includesKey: anId) ifFalse: [ self order add: anId ].
        self windows at: anId put: tool.
        "TELL WHOEVER ASKED (docs/appspec.md A6): the Tools menu registers a
         hook here so a tool that registers AFTER the menu was built — a user
         app, an AppVm window arriving with its first frame — appears in it
         without anyone remembering to add it. That is WINARMVM's own rule
         (`a tool registered in a later sprint appears without anyone
         remembering`), which its declarative menu got for free and our
         NSMenu needs a hook for. Fired on RE-registration too, and the
         hook's job includes ignoring ids it has already seen."
        RegisterHook isNil ifFalse: [ RegisterHook value: anId ].
        ^tool
    ]

    "The hook above. One block, not a list: the only subscriber is the
     platform's menu, and a second subscriber should force the conversation
     about what this is becoming rather than silently queue behind the first."
    AppToolWindow class >> onRegister: aBlock <Object> [ RegisterHook := aBlock. ^self ]

    AppToolWindow class >> named: anId <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        ^self windows at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> open: anId <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        | tool |
        tool := self named: anId.
        tool isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^tool open
    ]

    "FORGET ONE WINDOW — natives destroyed, registration dropped. The window
     of an app VM that has been retired must NOT survive its VM: a registration
     that outlives what was behind it is a shell that fronts on the next launch
     (`launchApp:` fronts a registered window rather than starting anything),
     showing a last frame whose clicks reach nobody. Unregistering is what makes
     relaunch mean START AGAIN."
    AppToolWindow class >> unregister: anId <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        | tool |
        tool := self windows at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        tool isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        [ tool teardown ] on: Error do: [ :e | nil ].
        self windows removeKey: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        self order remove: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    "For tests and for a fresh environment: forget every registration."
    AppToolWindow class >> reset [
        Windows := nil. Order := nil.
        EventDepth := nil. RerenderPending := nil. Suppress := nil.
        Reports := nil.
        self resetCounts.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the realizer, chosen once for the image ─────────────────────────

     Headless — a test, a worker with no UI, a `run` from the CLI — leaves this
     as the null realizer, and the ENTIRE pipeline still runs: normalize,
     validate, diff, ops, counters, damage. That is what makes A1 testable
     before A2's Cocoa realizer exists, and it is also what a worker VM with no
     display should do rather than fail."

    "IT RESOLVES ITSELF, and that is not cleverness — it is the only thing that
     works in both boot paths. Loading `world/*.mst` runs a file's trailing
     doits, but the GUI boots its VMs FROM THE IMAGE, and image boot replays
     only a two-entry `WORLD_DOITS` list (cocoa_gui/src/boot.rs): a
     `realizerClass: CocoaRealize.` line at the foot of the Cocoa file would
     simply never run there, which is exactly how it failed the first time.
     So instead of being told, this ASKS — late-bound by name, the same
     discipline CocoaUI uses to reach views that load after it
     (`(Worker classNamed: #CocoaMonitor) ifNotNil: [ :mc | mc register ]`).
     A VM that loaded the Cocoa layer finds CocoaRealize and draws; every
     other VM finds nothing and keeps the null realizer, which is the right
     answer for a headless image rather than a fallback from a broken one.
     Resolved once and cached, so the reflection sweep costs nothing after."
    AppToolWindow class >> realizerClass ^ <Object> [
        RealizerClass isNil ifTrue: [
            RealizerClass := (Worker classNamed: #CocoaRealize)
                ifNil: [ AppNullRealizer ] ].
        ^RealizerClass
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> realizerClass: aClass <Object> [
        RealizerClass := aClass.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the beat ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One clock for the whole registry rather than a timer per window: easier
     to reason about, and it makes `tick` a place every open window visits."

    AppToolWindow class >> tickAll [
        self order do: [ :k | | t |
            t := self windows at: k.
            t isOpen ifTrue: [ t tick ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> closeAll [
        self order do: [ :k | (self windows at: k) teardown ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── the dispatch discipline, shared by every window ─────────────────"

    AppToolWindow class >> eventDepth ^ <Integer> [
        ^EventDepth isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ EventDepth ]
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> suppressing ^ <Boolean> [ ^Suppress == true ]
    AppToolWindow class >> suppress: aBool <Boolean> [ Suppress := aBool. ^self ]

    AppToolWindow class >> enterEvent [ EventDepth := self eventDepth + 1. ^self ]
    AppToolWindow class >> leaveEvent [
        EventDepth := (self eventDepth - 1) max: 0.
        ^self
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> rerenderPending ^ <Boolean> [ ^RerenderPending == true ]
    AppToolWindow class >> rerenderPending: aBool <Boolean> [ RerenderPending := aBool. ^self ]

    "── the metrics, because an id strategy fails QUIETLY ───────────────"

    AppToolWindow class >> patchCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^PatchCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ PatchCount ]
    ]
    AppToolWindow class >> rebuildCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^RebuildCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ RebuildCount ]
    ]
    AppToolWindow class >> countPatch [ PatchCount := self patchCount + 1. ^self ]
    AppToolWindow class >> countRebuild [ RebuildCount := self rebuildCount + 1. ^self ]
    AppToolWindow class >> resetCounts [ PatchCount := 0. RebuildCount := 0. ^self ]

    "The bounding box of two {x. y. w. h} rects — how damage coalesces between
     paints."
    AppToolWindow class >> union: a <Array> with: b <Array> ^ <Array> [
        | x0 y0 x1 y1 |
        x0 := (a at: 1) min: (b at: 1).
        y0 := (a at: 2) min: (b at: 2).
        x1 := ((a at: 1) + (a at: 3)) max: ((b at: 1) + (b at: 3)).
        y1 := ((a at: 2) + (a at: 4)) max: ((b at: 2) + (b at: 4)).
        ^Array with: x0 with: y0 with: x1 - x0 with: y1 - y0
    ]

    "── where a tool's own failure goes ─────────────────────────────────

     A tool must not be able to take the environment down by raising in its
     render or handler block, and it must not fail SILENTLY either. Reports are
     kept in a list the tests can read, and a platform realizer is free to
     mirror them into a transcript."

    AppToolWindow class >> reports ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        Reports isNil ifTrue: [ Reports := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Reports
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> report: aString <String> [
        self reports add: aString.
        ^self
    ]

    AppToolWindow class >> clearReports [ Reports := nil. ^self ]

    "── an instance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    | id title width height renderBlock handlerBlock paintBlock realizer lastSpec damage closeBlock tickBlock |

    setId: anId <Symbol> title: aTitle <String> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer>
    render: r <Object> handler: hh <Object> paint: pp <Object> ^ <AppToolWindow> [
        id := anId.
        title := aTitle.
        width := w.
        height := h.
        renderBlock := r.
        handlerBlock := hh.
        paintBlock := pp.
        damage := Dictionary new.
        lastSpec := nil.
        realizer := nil.
        ^self
    ]

    id ^ <Symbol> [ ^id ]
    title ^ <String> [ ^title ]
    title: aString <String> [ title := aString. ^self ]
    width ^ <Integer> [ ^width ]
    height ^ <Integer> [ ^height ]
    lastSpec ^ <Object> [ ^lastSpec ]
    paintBlock ^ <Object> [ ^paintBlock ]

    "Built on first use, so registering a tool costs no realizer either."
    realizer ^ <Object> [
        realizer isNil ifTrue: [
            realizer := AppToolWindow realizerClass newFor: self ].
        ^realizer
    ]

    isOpen ^ <Boolean> [ ^self realizer isOpen ]

    "── open: front or build, which is the Mac's own choice, kept ───────"

    open ^ <Boolean> [
        (self realizer open) ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        lastSpec isNil ifTrue: [ ^self realize ].
        ^self rerender
    ]

    "CLOSE IS HIDE, NEVER DIE — for an IN-PROCESS tool, whose state is class-side
     in this very image: it outlives the window, so reopening is instant and
     loses nothing. That is the default and it is bound here so no tool can
     forget it.

     A WINDOW BACKED BY ANOTHER VM IS THE OTHER CASE, and hiding it is wrong:
     the state is not here, it is a live VM with a thread, a timer and an inbox,
     and hiding leaves it publishing frames at a window nobody can see, forever.
     So close is a HOOK. Whoever owns what is behind the window says what close
     means — AppVmDisplay retires the app (docs/process_services.md's rule that
     every lifecycle transition has exactly one owner); a tool that sets no hook
     gets hide, exactly as before."
    onClose: aBlock <Object> [ closeBlock := aBlock. ^self ]

    closeRequested ^ <Object> [
        closeBlock isNil ifFalse: [ ^closeBlock value ].
        self realizer hide.
        ^self
    ]

    teardown [
        self realizer teardown.
        lastSpec := nil.
        damage := Dictionary new.
        ^self
    ]

    "── damage: the patch discipline, extended to pane pixels ───────────

     A pane's contents are pixels, so no diff of a spec can ever say they
     changed — but the HANDLER knows exactly which cell it touched, and that
     information was in the system all along. A handler that changes what a
     pane shows SAYS SO; painting takes the damage, so it cannot linger or
     loop. Minimal update is the FEATURE of a patching UI, not an optimisation
     of one."

    invalidatePane: anId <Object> [
        "The whole pane. nil damage MEANS everything, and it absorbs any region
         already recorded — whole beats parts, and cannot be shrunk back."
        damage at: anId asString put: nil.
        ^self
    ]

    invalidatePane: anId <Object> rect: r <Array> [
        "A region, {x. y. w. h} in the pane's OWN pixels — the same space its
         events arrive in. Regions union into one bounding box between paints:
         between two paints there is rarely more than one event, and a box is
         exact for the case that matters."
        | k |
        k := anId asString.
        (damage includesKey: k)
            ifTrue: [
                (damage at: k) isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
                damage at: k put: (AppToolWindow union: (damage at: k) with: r) ]
            ifFalse: [ damage at: k put: r ].
        ^self
    ]

    "THE HANDLE A PAINT BLOCK DRAWS THROUGH — `AppPixels planeFor: (tool
     paneHandle: #grid) …`. Named `paneHwnd:` as well, below, because that is
     what WINARMVM's tools already say and this is the one place the two
     vocabularies have to meet; the number means `which surface`, not `which
     window`, on either platform."
    paneHandle: anId <Object> ^ <Integer> [
        ^self realizer paneHandleFor: anId
    ]

    paneHwnd: anId <Object> ^ <Integer> [ ^self paneHandle: anId ]

    damageFor: anId <Object> ^ <Object> [
        "#clean when nothing is pending, nil for the whole pane, a rect for a
         region. Named for tests, which is why it does not answer nil for both."
        ^damage at: anId asString ifAbsent: [ #clean ]
    ]

    invalidateAllPanes [
        "A rebuild replaced the pane surfaces, so everything they showed is
         gone whether or not anyone said so."
        lastSpec isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self panesIn: lastSpec do: [ :node | damage at: node idOrEmpty put: nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    panesIn: aNode <Object> do: aBlock <Object> [
        (aNode isKindOf: AppNode) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        (AppSpec paneTypes includes: aNode type) ifTrue: [ aBlock value: aNode ].
        aNode children do: [ :c | self panesIn: c do: aBlock ].
        ^self
    ]

    paintDirty [
        "Take the damage FIRST and clear it, so a paint block that invalidates
         (it should not, but an error handler might) cannot loop this."
        | pending |
        damage isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        pending := damage.
        damage := Dictionary new.
        paintBlock isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        pending keysAndValuesDo: [ :paneId :region |
            [ paintBlock value: self value: paneId value: region ]
                on: Error do: [ :e | AppToolWindow report: e messageText ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── the beat ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    "A tool that wants to move on its own — a clock, a monitor, anything whose
     state changes without a click — registers a block here and it runs on the
     shared beat. The block decides whether anything actually changed; the beat
     is not a repaint, and a tool that rerenders on every one of them makes the
     differ do work for nothing.

     A raising tick is REPORTED AND DISARMED, not left to fire forever: a
     broken block on a 4Hz beat would otherwise fill the transcript several
     times a second and bury the first report, which is the one that says
     what went wrong."
    onTick: aBlock <Object> [ tickBlock := aBlock. ^self ]

    tick [
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        AppToolWindow suppressing ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        tickBlock isNil ifFalse: [
            [ tickBlock value ]
                on: Error do: [ :e |
                    tickBlock := nil.
                    AppToolWindow report:
                        'tool ' , id printString , ' tick disarmed: ' , e messageText ] ].
        self realizer tick.
        ^self
    ]

    "── dispatch: depth in, handler, depth out ──────────────────────────

     Only at depth zero does a pending rerender actually happen, which is what
     turns N state changes in one handler into one diff and one screen update."

    dispatch: event <Symbol> value: aValue <Object> ^ <Object> [
        handlerBlock isNil ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        AppToolWindow suppressing ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        AppToolWindow enterEvent.
        [ handlerBlock value: event value: aValue ]
            on: Error do: [ :e | AppToolWindow report: e messageText ].
        AppToolWindow leaveEvent.
        (AppToolWindow eventDepth = 0 and: [ AppToolWindow rerenderPending ])
            ifTrue: [
                AppToolWindow rerenderPending: false.
                self rerender ].
        ^0
    ]

    "── rerender: the whole programming model in one method ─────────────"

    rerender ^ <Boolean> [
        | spec ops |
        "THE DEPTH GUARD COMES FIRST, before asking whether there is even a
         window. The discipline is about WHEN a reconcile may happen, not about
         whether one is worth doing — and a deferred rerender that finds a
         closed window at depth zero simply answers false, which costs nothing
         and keeps one rule instead of two."
        AppToolWindow eventDepth > 0 ifTrue: [
            AppToolWindow rerenderPending: true.
            ^true ].
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        spec := self currentSpec.
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        lastSpec isNil ifTrue: [ ^self realizeSpec: spec ].
        ops := AppSpec diff: lastSpec against: spec.
        ops isNil ifTrue: [ ^self realizeSpec: spec ].
        ops isEmpty ifTrue: [
            "NO OPS IS NOT NOTHING TO DO. A tool that painted a pane changed
             what is on screen without changing a single prop — a pencil stroke
             is exactly that. `nothing changed` means nothing changed about the
             CONTROLS; what changed about the PANES is whatever the handler
             invalidated, and only that is painted."
            lastSpec := spec.
            self paintDirty.
            ^true ].
        ^self apply: ops spec: spec
    ]

    currentSpec ^ <Object> [
        | spec bad |
        renderBlock isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        spec := [ renderBlock value ] on: Error do: [ :e |
            AppToolWindow report: e messageText. nil ].
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        spec := AppSpec normalize: spec.
        bad := AppSpec validate: spec.
        bad isNil ifFalse: [
            "A malformed spec is refused HERE, where the tool that built it can
             still be named, rather than half-realized into a window."
            AppToolWindow report: 'tool ' , id printString , ': ' , bad.
            ^nil ].
        ^spec
    ]

    "── the two outcomes ────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    realize ^ <Boolean> [
        | spec |
        spec := self currentSpec.
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^self realizeSpec: spec
    ]

    realizeSpec: spec <AppNode> ^ <Boolean> [
        | places |
        AppToolWindow countRebuild.
        AppToolWindow suppress: true.
        self realizer destroyControls.
        places := AppSpecLayout
            layout: spec width: self realizer clientWidth metrics: self realizer metrics.
        places do: [ :p | self realizer createFor: p ].
        "EVERY CONTROL IS PLACED; NOW THE PLATFORM MAY FIT ITSELF TO THEM. A
         tool declares its window size against the metrics of the platform it
         was written on, so a tool ported from the other one is always a little
         wrong here — too tall, or clipped at the right edge. The realizer has
         just seen the real extent, so it is the thing that can say so; a
         realizer with nothing to adjust ignores this."
        self realizer layoutFinished: places.
        AppToolWindow suppress: false.
        lastSpec := spec.
        self invalidateAllPanes.
        self paintDirty.
        ^true
    ]

    apply: ops <OrderedCollection> spec: spec <AppNode> ^ <Boolean> [
        "IN PLACE, or not at all. Only prop patches are applied here; anything
         structural falls back to a rebuild, which is honest and is exactly
         what the rebuild counter is for — it says when that stops being good
         enough."
        | ok |
        ok := true.
        ops do: [ :op |
            ((op at: 1) == #setNodeProps or: [ (op at: 1) == #setWindowProps ])
                ifFalse: [ ok := false ] ].
        ok ifFalse: [ ^self realizeSpec: spec ].
        AppToolWindow suppress: true.
        ops do: [ :op |
            (op at: 1) == #setWindowProps
                ifTrue: [ self applyWindowProps: (op at: 2) ]
                ifFalse: [ self realizer applyNodeProps: (op at: 3) to: (op at: 2) ] ].
        AppToolWindow suppress: false.
        AppToolWindow countPatch.
        lastSpec := spec.
        self paintDirty.
        ^true
    ]

    applyWindowProps: changed <OrderedCollection> [
        changed do: [ :a | a key == #title ifTrue: [ title := a value ] ].
        self realizer applyWindowProps: changed.
        ^self
    ]
]
